biryani is a perfectly cooked rice dish  seasoned with whole spices and layered  between tender chunks of meat and  streams of delicious gravy it's a dish  that will give you the perfect bite each  time  today we're gonna make our favorite  biryani recipe that is sure to keep  everyone you know coming back for more  the origin of biryani is debatable some  say it originated in iran making its way  across india through trade routes others  say it originated in tamil nadu one  thing everyone can agree on however is  that biryani is delicious almost every  region of south asia has their own style  of biryani hyderabad's version is extra  saucy and flavorful in tamil ambur  biryani is made with mint yogurt  marinade and in lucknow they sometimes  use stock to create a richer flavor  today we're going to be making bombay  style biryani it's a little sweet and  topped with crispy fried onions let's  start with the meat there are many  protein options when it comes to making  biryani like goat lamb shrimp and  chicken for our recipe we're going with  chicken since it's the most traditional  we're using bone-in chicken thighs and  drumsticks boneless chicken works too  but bone-in will help to give us a more  flavorful dish to incorporate even more  flavor in our chicken we're going to  marinate it we tested both a spice based  marinade and a yogurt-based marinade we  found that marinating with yogurt is key  to getting creamy tender chicken so  that's what we're going with full fat  yogurt works best in a large bowl  combine the yogurt ginger paste garlic  paste salt cumin coriander garam masala  chili powder and lime juice we'll add  the chicken and toss to coat well  then cover the bowl with plastic wrap  and marinate for one hour at room  temperature or up to three hours in the  refrigerator  now let's prepare our rice  for this recipe we will be using basmati  it's fragrant slightly nutty and will  pair really nicely with our mix of  spices we'll start by gently rinsing the  rice in cool water  repeat this a few times until the water  runs clear  now we'll cover the rice with fresh  water and let it soak for 30 to 45  minutes  not only does soaking the rice help to  remove excess starch and speed up the  cooking process but for fragrant rice  like basmati and jasmine this will also  help strengthen their natural aroma and  flavors  once the rice has finished soaking we'll  move on to the cooking process to a pot  of water add cardamom pods a cinnamon  stick  bay leaf cloves and salt  when that comes to a boil we'll add our  rice the key to perfectly cooked biryani  is to cook the rice until it's only  about three quarters of the way done so  it's still al dente this should only  take about four minutes  drain  then add the rice to a plate or baking  sheet and spread in an even layer to  cool like this  now that our rice is done we can move on  to our next three ingredients that will  make our biryani extra special  onions prunes and potatoes we'll first  boil the potatoes separately until  almost tender  if we cooked them with the meat instead  we'd end up with a mushy texture in the  final dish  no thanks  once our potatoes are softened we're  going to fry them in a neutral oil to  provide texture to our dish and the  crunch doesn't stop there  next we're going to fry some onions a  must when it comes to bombay style  biryani we recommend using a small red  onion or shallot  we'll fry them until they're nice and  golden brown seriously look how good  those look  before we move on to layering our  biryani we're going to make some saffron  water  saffron adds a luxurious scent flavor  and color to our biryani  we know it can be pricey so if you don't  have saffron on hand use turmeric to get  that orange hue  we're going to pour hot water over our  saffron threads and then gently crush  them with the back of a spoon to get all  of that flavor out  now let's make our gravy we tested a few  different oils for our gravy and found  that a mixture of oil and ghee or  clarified butter creates the best flavor  without being too rich we'll heat our  oil mixture until it shimmers then we're  going to add all of our whole spices to  get every last drop of flavor out of  them  we want to fry them until they are  fragrant not burnt about one to two  minutes  then we'll add our chopped onions and  saute  once the onions begin to brown add the  tomatoes and cook for three to five  minutes  then add the marinated chicken a splash  of water and salt cover and let this  cook for 10 minutes  we'll add the potatoes back in and then  add our special ingredient prunes prunes  are used in bombay style biryani to add  an element of sweetness to the dish  now that our gravy is finished let's  move on to layering when it comes to  cooking biryani there are a few ways to  do it  you can cook it dum style which involves  layering raw ingredients in a heavy  bottomed pot and slow cooking them all  together  or you can make it pakka style which  means cooking all of the components  separately and then layering them  together after  we're cooking our biryani pakka style  because it's more traditional for bombay  style biryani and it ensures we don't  overcook any of the elements in the dish  we'll start by reserving half of the  beautiful gravy and add half of our rice  into the pot  then we'll sprinkle some of our saffron  water on top  we'll add some herbs a little ghee and  our fried onions as finishing touches  for the first layer and then we'll  repeat the whole process for the second  layer  once we have our biryani all piled up  we're going to cover the pot with foil  and seal it really tightly  traditionally we'd wrap the rim with  dough to get the tight seal we're  looking for but today we're going to  keep it simple by using foil  once it's done cooking we'll leave it  covered to rest for 10 minutes to let  the steam really fluff up the rice and  finish cooking  and there you have it your beautifully  layered bombay biryani  with biryani every bit of time taken to  perfect this dish really pays off we're  going to serve our biryani today with a  little onion raita and pickled mango we  know that nothing will beat your mom's  biryani but we think this recipe comes  very close  you
 

